    path: "inbox/demo/00 Start Here.md",
    body: "# Start here: ZenNotes feature tour\n\nThis folder is a guided demo vault for ZenNotes as it exists today. It covers markdown rendering, keyboard-first workflows, search, views, settings, and the vault-level features that sit on top of plain files.\n\n## How to use this tour\n\n- Open notes in **Edit**, **Split**, and **Preview** to see where each feature is most useful.\n- Use `Space p` or the outline panel on longer notes.\n- Use `Space f` to search notes by title and path.\n- Use `Space s t` to fuzzy-search text across the vault.\n- Open **Help** from the footer or type `:help` from normal mode for the built-in manual.\n- Try `⌘.` to toggle **Zen mode** while reading any note here.\n\n## The tour\n\n1. [[01 Markdown Basics]]: headings, emphasis, lists, links, frontmatter, slash commands, and formatting a selection\n2. [[02 Code Blocks]]: fenced code, syntax highlighting, and languages you add yourself\n3. [[03 Tables and Task Lists]]: editable tables, task states, fields, subtasks, and the Tasks list\n4. [[04 Math with KaTeX]]: inline and display math, numbered equations, and Typst as an alternative\n5. [[05 Mermaid Diagrams]]: flow, sequence, state, gantt, and pie diagrams, drawn in your theme\n6. [[05b Math Diagrams]]: TikZ, JSXGraph, and function-plot for paper-grade figures, interactive geometry, and quick plots\n7. [[05c D2 Diagrams]]: D2 architecture diagrams, with nested containers, data models, and sequences\n8. [[06 Callouts and Footnotes]]: callouts, footnotes, highlights, images, sizes, video embeds, and local files\n9. [[07 Wiki Links and Tags]]: wikilinks, block links, embeds, tags, backlinks, and the Atlas map\n10. [[08 Daily Notes]]: daily, weekly, and monthly notes, quick capture, and date shortcuts\n11. [[09 Vim Cheat Sheet]]: the app-specific motions, leader flows, folds, and ex commands\n12. [[10 Ideas and Tasks]]: a realistic note that composes multiple features at once\n13. [[11 Workspace, Search, and Views]]: home, tabs, splits, panels, comments, archive, trash, and session restore\n14. [[12 Settings and Keymaps]]: themes, CSS overrides, editor options, remappable keys, and `config.toml`\n15. [[13 Commands, Help, and Demo Tour]]: command palette discovery, ex commands, built-in Help, PDF export, and this tour\n16. [[14 Reference Pane and Floating Windows]]: pinned notes, research context, and detached note windows\n17. [[15 Search Backends and Fuzzy Workflows]]: note search, vault text search, find in a note, fzf, and ripgrep\n18. [[16 Tasks Calendar and Kanban]]: the Tasks calendar, Kanban boards, saved filters, and whole notes as tasks\n19. [[17 Templates, Drawings, and Databases]]: templates, Excalidraw drawings, and spreadsheet-like databases\n20. [[18 Workflows]]: repeatable changes across many notes, planned before anything is written\n21. [[19 Cloud, CLI, and AI Agents]]: ZenNotes Cloud, the tray, the `zn` CLI, and assistants over MCP\n\n## What this demo folder covers\n\nZenNotes is more than a markdown renderer. Across this folder you can try:\n\n- plain file-based notes with no hidden database\n- live preview plus dedicated preview and split modes\n- heading and list folding and outline jumps\n- wikilinks, block links, embeds, tags, backlinks, and the Atlas map\n- quick capture from anywhere, and Quick Notes\n- Inbox, Archive, and Trash as separate lifecycle stages\n- vault-wide Tasks as a list, a calendar, and Kanban boards\n- templates, Excalidraw drawings, and databases\n- note search, vault text search, and find in a note\n- Mermaid, D2, TikZ, JSXGraph, and function-plot diagrams\n- KaTeX or Typst math\n- slash commands, `@` dates, and text replacements\n- comments on selected text\n- Vim mode, leader hints, hint mode, ex commands, and pane motion\n- themes, CSS overrides, keymaps, and a portable `config.toml`\n- workflows that plan before they write\n- ZenNotes Cloud, the `zn` CLI, and AI assistants over MCP\n- session restore for panes, tabs, built-in views, and window bounds\n\n## The point\n\nEvery file here is ordinary markdown on disk. Open the folder in ZenNotes, `vim`, VS Code, or another markdown editor and the notes are still yours.\n^portable\n\n#demo #reference #tour\n"
